- the Gaussian mode shape and truncation cost about 1.5×. Here $D$ is the aperture diameter (20 mm) and $w_0=0.45D$ the Gaussian beam's waist, its 1/e² intensity radius at the aperture (0.45 is the model's chosen waist-to-aperture ratio, §C10.3b). The factor $t=0.915$ is the one-way power transmission of a Gaussian with $w_0=0.45D$ through the aperture, so $t^2=0.84$ covers transmit and receive. In the far field $A_{\rm mode}\to t^2\pi w_0^2/2$, so the hard area $\pi(D/2)^2$ exceeds $A_{\rm mode}$ by $2\times(D/2)^2/(t^2w_0^2)=2\times1/(4\times0.45^2\times0.84)=2\times1.47=2.9$, i.e. polarization (2) times mode shape and truncation (1.47);
- the rest is near-field loss, because a 20 mm aperture is larger than the confocal optimum at short range.

So even a perfectly aligned monostatic coherent receiver uses only about 1/4 to 1/11 of the light its lens intercepts, which is one reason its photon budget is tighter than ToF's.
